<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guangdong</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guangdong is a coastal province of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>, and the most populous province of the country. Part of the historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingnan">Lingnan</a>, it was a centre of maritime trade for over a thousand years, the base of the Southern Han kingdom in the 10th century and the gateway for European trade under the Qing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canton-system">Canton System</a>. Since 1980 special economic zones such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenzhen">Shenzhen</a> have made it China's largest provincial economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guangdong.jpg" alt="Guangdong" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charlie fong · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China_Resources_Headquarters%26Shenzhen_Bay_gymnasium_in_Nanshan_District2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Guangdong faces the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> on the south and has 4,300 km of coastline. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leizhou-peninsula">Leizhou Peninsula</a> lies at its south-western end. The Pearl River Delta, where the East River, North River and West River converge, is filled with hundreds of small islands. A few mountain ranges collectively called the Nan Mountains separate the province from the north, and its highest peak is Shikengkong, at about 1,900 metres. The name means east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a>: the Chinese character guang, meaning wide, has been linked with the region since Guang Prefecture was created in 226, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> the Two Guangs were separated into Guangnan East and Guangnan West circuits.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> began in the Pearl River Delta about 7,000 years ago, and in the north of the province the Shixia culture dates from about 4600 to 4200 years ago. The region was originally inhabited by a mixture of tribal groups known to the Chinese as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a>, the Hundred Yue. It first became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a>, which after unifying China expanded southwards and set up Nanhai Commandery at Panyu, near present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>. Chinese administration and reliable historical records began with the Qin. An independent kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a>, then controlled the region between the fall of Qin and the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a>.</p><p>The Han administered Guangdong, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozhi-province">Jiaozhi Province</a>, and its southern part was a gateway for traders from the west, as far away as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. Under the Wu Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a>, Guangdong was made its own province, Guang Province, in 226. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingnan">Lingnan</a>, immigrants from the Central Plains moved in and formed a local culture of its own, whose languages, music, cuisine and opera spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p><p>In the early 1920s Guangdong was the staging area where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> prepared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-expedition">Northern Expedition</a> against the warlords, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whampoa-military-academy">Whampoa Military Academy</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> trained its commanders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>, born in the province, declared a military government there. At the end of the Chinese Civil War Guangzhou was briefly the Kuomintang's provisional capital before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> took the province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Guangdong is the most populous province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, with a population of 126,012,510 in the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> is its capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenzhen">Shenzhen</a>, the first special economic zone, lies in the Pearl River Delta, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> borders the province to the south. The Cantonese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a> and Teochew make up much of the Han population, and Guangdong is the traditional heartland of Yue Chinese. It is the ancestral home of many overseas Chinese, including most of the 19th-century railroad labourers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guangdong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Liangzhu was extremely influential: its sphere of influence reached as far north as Shanxi and as far south as <b>Guangdong</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/liangzhu-culture">Liangzhu culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1837 Hong Huoxiu, a Hakka from a poor village in <b>Guangdong</b>, failed the imperial examination for the third time and suffered a breakdown, in which he dreamed of visiting Heaven and meeting a celestial family; his heavenly father gave him the name Hong Xiuquan.</blockquote><a href="/movement/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Mac Cuu, a Ming loyalist from <b>Guangdong</b>, settled at Ha Tien, then a Cambodian coastal town called Peam, in the late 17th century and developed it into a trading port with Cambodian approval.</blockquote><a href="/state/ha-tien-principality">Ha Tien Principality</a></li><li><blockquote>Luo Fangbo, a Hakka scholar and migrant from <b>Guangdong</b>, organised Chinese gold miners near Pontianak and in 1777 founded the Lanfang kongsi at Mandor.</blockquote><a href="/state/lanfang-republic">Lanfang Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Southern Han controlled modern <b>Guangdong</b> and Guangxi and grew rich on the sea trade with South-east Asia and the Indian Ocean, handled by Arab, Persian and other foreign merchants at Guangzhou.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-han">Southern Han</a></li><li><blockquote>Hong Xiuquan, a Hakka from <b>Guangdong</b> who repeatedly failed the civil service examinations, came to believe from visions and Christian tracts that he was the younger brother of Jesus.</blockquote><a href="/state/taiping-heavenly-kingdom">Taiping Heavenly Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
